WEBVTT

00:00.250 --> 00:02.848
Right now, your architecture probably
lives in your head.

00:03.374 --> 00:06.450
StackHatch lets you see it, question it,
and shape it.

00:07.000 --> 00:08.586
Say all you have is an idea.

00:09.114 --> 00:12.670
Open a blank canvas and describe it the
way you would to a colleague.

00:16.400 --> 00:18.972
A few seconds later there's a first
draft, and you

00:18.972 --> 00:21.647
can click into any part of it to see why
it's there.

00:26.000 --> 00:27.842
Or maybe the code's already written.

00:28.421 --> 00:31.023
Paste its GitHub link, and StackHatch
reads

00:31.023 --> 00:33.504
the repository and draws the map for
you.

00:34.300 --> 00:35.862
Then you can ask it questions.

00:40.850 --> 00:43.900
It works on private repos too, with a
read-only

00:43.900 --> 00:46.691
GitHub token that only ever goes to
GitHub.

00:48.050 --> 00:51.684
If you've already written a spec, drop
the Markdown file in, and

00:51.684 --> 00:55.090
your requirements turn into a map you
can review and refine.

00:57.600 --> 01:00.219
Every requirement ends up with a home on
the map.

01:04.000 --> 01:06.126
And when you're not sure about a choice,

01:06.126 --> 01:08.518
click the component and ask for
alternatives.

01:10.740 --> 01:14.116
You see the trade-offs, and if one fits
better, you swap it in.

01:19.400 --> 01:23.219
And if you'd rather do it yourself, you
can add components, connect

01:23.219 --> 01:27.095
them, lock the parts you've settled on,
and let auto-layout tidy up.

01:31.250 --> 01:35.847
When it's time to share, you can export
an image, SVG, JSON or YAML.

01:36.100 --> 01:39.217
Or have it write a product requirements
document from the map.

01:44.550 --> 01:47.834
It all runs on your own key, from
Anthropic,

01:47.834 --> 01:50.520
OpenAI, Google Gemini or OpenRouter.

01:50.900 --> 01:53.711
There's no account and no StackHatch
server in between.

01:54.152 --> 01:55.743
Your maps stay in your browser.

01:58.600 --> 01:59.526
That's StackHatch.

01:59.865 --> 02:03.553
It's free and open source, so go try it
at stackhatch.io.
